<DocsLayout
  title="Transaction Grouping"
  description="Understand atomic transaction grouping, XID boundaries, and statement sequences."
  currentSlug="transactions"
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    In raw MySQL binlogs, a single logical transaction (e.g. updating an order, deducting inventory, and inserting an audit log) is written across dozens of individual low-level events: <code>QUERY (BEGIN)</code>, <code>TABLE_MAP</code>, <code>UPDATE_ROWS</code>, <code>WRITE_ROWS</code>, and finally an <code>XID</code> (or <code>COMMIT</code>) event.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transaction-boundaries">How MySQL Groups Transactions</h2>
  <p>
    Scanning binlogs linearly makes it nearly impossible to tell which row changes belonged to which transaction. Binsight reconstructs logical transactions automatically by tracking:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>GTID (Global Transaction Identifier):</strong> For clusters running with GTID mode enabled, every transaction is grouped by its unique UUID + sequence number.</li>
    <li><strong>Anonymous Transactions (XID):</strong> For non-GTID setups, Binsight groups all events starting from <code>BEGIN</code> up to the terminating <code>XID_EVENT</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="xid-boundaries">XID & Two-Phase Commit Boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>XID</code> event marks the exact instant a transaction achieved two-phase commit durability on the InnoDB storage engine. Binsight correlates all table maps and row mutations that shared the same internal transaction ID:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`Transaction #918239 (2 statements, 4 row mutations)
  ├── TABLE_MAP #108 -> shop.orders
  ├── UPDATE_ROWS (1 row modified)
  ├── TABLE_MAP #109 -> shop.order_items
  ├── WRITE_ROWS (3 rows inserted)
  └── XID: 918239 (COMMITTED in 14ms)`}
    lang="text"
    title="Transaction Timeline"
  />

  <h2 id="grouped-view">Flat View vs. Grouped View</h2>
  <p>
    In the Binsight web console, press the <kbd class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-[#161f2c] border border-[#2d3b4f] text-[#38bdf8] font-mono text-xs">t</kbd> key to instantly toggle between:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flat View:</strong> Shows every individual raw binlog event sequentially, ideal for checking protocol-level markers like <code>FORMAT_DESCRIPTION</code>, <code>ROTATE</code>, and <code>HEARTBEAT</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Grouped View:</strong> Collapses all events into expandable transaction cards showing total rows affected, execution latency, and table distribution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rolled-back-txns">Identifying Rolled-Back Transactions</h2>
  <p>
    When an explicit <code>ROLLBACK</code> occurs in InnoDB, transactional row changes are not written to the binlog. However, changes to non-transactional tables (such as MyISAM or temporary tables) modified within a rolled-back transaction <em>are</em> recorded. Binsight flags these anomalies with a warning pill.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
